et what  you need you get sqrt  and the brackets you need to move your  cursor in here so you can start typing  that  once you come outside the box it'll show  you what  it thinks you've written so students  here will often  oh they missed the parentheses and the  45 is only on half of this  expression and they realize they better  put some parentheses in here to get what  they need  students have been pretty capable of  figuring out how to make their answers  look like the way that they need them to  and write the math that they need to  we need to determine whether what the  student wrote is  correct one of the things that  we kind of finesse past the students but  would like a solution to  is sometimes mathematica is too good  we ask the students what the sign of pi  over 6 is  and i'm hoping that they've memorized  the unit circle and they'll answer one  half  they could type in 0.5 that would be  fine  but i can make them be specific on  sine of pi over 3. they need square root  3 over 2 they can't use a decimal  approximation but  they could also just type in the  question they could answer sine of pi  over 6 and mathematically would know  yes the sine of pi over 6 is one-half  and that does match what the professor  wrote down as the answer  and so we're looking for some ways that  we can  specify certain forms that are allowed  on certain types of problems  and here's the grade report the student  gets back  it shows exactly what they typed in  it shows how the answer got interpreted  notice that mathematica did do this much  the square root of 32  got simplified to write a better  expression  this is sometimes a good teaching moment  for the students they see that what's  parsed here is different than what they  typed  and they may now realize oh yeah there  are rules for  simplifying square roots this winds up  being a pretty good teaching tool as  well  compared to the correct answer and they  can see what their mistake is  what types of things can we ask students  to do  we can ask them to input answers which  are numbers  which for me in pre-calculus i by and  large mean a real number  but it works equally well for complex  numbers  research-wise i did my phd thesis in  combinatorial game theory  so just for fun i think it would be kind  of cool to  um john conway donald knuth  surreal numbers and berla camp conway  and  guy the the games if we could do  computations on games as a bigger set  the numbers i think that would be pretty  cool  but that's just for fun for the teaching  purposes  mathematical functions are important and  it can tell whether one function is  equal to another function  you can use text strings as  the way that you're evaluating the  answer though that's not very useful  the answer might be x plus two and the  student types in  x plus two and it sure did sound like i  said the right answer but you didn't  realize that i left a little pause in  there  if the cursor isn't in the right spot  and there's an invisible space  showing up in the character string then  it gets counted wrong so  we don't use that very often  we do have the ability to take  multi-part  answers so if the answer is a set  we can handle that case though it's not  quite as nice as we'd like it to be  we don't use the squiggly braces  the way that students would typically  write sets you just separate things with  commas and they  have to learn a little bit of syntax  there and it doesn't  look like it typically would the the way  a typical  student would typically write it out  some things that are  on my wish list i'd like to be able to  ask  students questions where their answer is  an infinite set  find all the angles where the sign is  one-half i'd like the students to be  able to  list a couple of answers that get us  started  tell us that the pattern repeats every  so often and then evaluate whether that  set  matches the answer key i've seen  a couple of presentations this week that  were very good  about examineer and a function that's  being loaded into the mathematica  platform  that would make the um  answer field a little bit more robust in  terms of how it evaluates things  there are certain cases like  factoring questions where maybe you  factor  out a negative one and then write down  the other factors  and we need to propose both  possible ways of factoring because the  two solutions are going to look  differently if you give me a list of  factors without the negative one and you  give me a list of factors with the  negative one  the factor lists are going to be  different and mathematic is going to  realize that the factors are different  but they are both correct factorizations  of the same polynomial  so um there's uh one thing on our wish  list that we're  hoping to get in the future  and then the final step um it turns out  there are some issues here originally i  thought  how hard can this be just spit it out  into a spreadsheet  uh turns out the database is probably  the preferable way to do it  and we have a very good database there  actually are  quite a few issues on the gradebook side  mostly dealing with security  but for the most part that's on the side  of the institution to  to work with the data security people  in terms of the instructors knowing how  the students have performed  and the students knowing how well  they've performed  it is as simple as just recording the  scores in a database and presenting that  data  how are we currently using this  our placement exam  about 7 000 students annually we've been  running it for  five years now we're approaching 40 000  students that have taken our placement  exam  the reporting goes directly into our  advisors hands and  they can seamlessly place students  into their first math class at the  university of minnesota  we use it in our pre-calculus classes  currently 600 students a year  and it saves our teaching assistants a  lot of time they're no longer having to  collect and grade  this routine homework students get  better feedback because they're getting  instant  feedback teaching assistants can spend  more of their time  working with more involved questions and  giving students feedback on their  on their writing we are  using this as a way to  prepare the students that are in our  sequence  they may need some help refreshing  themselves on  pre-calculus topics this review material  is available to the students they can  go back and review the topics they need  help with as they're getting ready for  calculus and it really has been  a benefit to us both in the spring  and this semester we've needed to move  away  from in-person pencil and paper exams  and this system has given us an  alternative  to delivering exams to  students in a reasonable way that  students  can show us what they know and not have  to deal with a lot of  technology concerns  the future we  welcome partnerships with others that  are interested in these same types of  things  in particular a lot has been done in  open educational resources and elsewhere  to work on delivering content  but they typically are lacking  the assessments they're typically  lacking the problem sets  so we would like to find ways that we  can share  the questions that we've written with  others and if we can  share with them crowdsource the content  so that we can get a lot of content  and deliver to students we think that  would be a worthwhile project  encouraged by some of the comments i've  heard this week  about the k-12 market i really think  this is a  a market that um really is wide open  for um for innovation  41 states in the united states have  the common core and that really is the  issue  k through 12 is aligning the assessments  to  to the common core standards and  benchmarking everything  and then there are a few other states  that go off and do  what they want to most noticeably is my  state minnesota  which is fairly notorious for kind of  going off and doing their own thing  but we have something similar to the  common core and aligning to those  standards is something that our k-12  schools need  and um working uh working to build  develop things in that field i think is  a  is a is a big opportunity as i mentioned  before  sometimes we need to ask for a specific  form from students to disallow them  using  functions allow them to use addition  subtraction multiplication division  square roots but not allow them to use  the sine function in their answer  mentioned also before these other issues  of  it'll be great to have the answer field  be able to  parse things out in terms of a variety  of different answers so if the student  says this we're going to give them half  credit  if the in a probability question if they  got the denominator right we'll give  them half credit  um and  other forms of the answer being  acceptable  the ability to write infinite sets i  think  provides an interesting programming  challenge  and we're busy working on  solutions to that so with that  i would like to thank everybody  for attending my contact  information is here and randall has  been monitoring the  chat window here if you have any  questions about this system  please please ask  the only thing that's been posted is  nam joshi says many k-12 schools use  google classroom and canvas  yeah there's some there's always the  issues  one of the other big issues with the  schools is they want this lms  integration we have not bothered to  to do that integration yet um but  but that certainly is um is an  issue for um making this work large  scale  um like i said minnesota is currently a  canvas school  um it isn't all that difficult to  uh to transfer this data back and forth  um so i think that's um that's  definitely a uh  a problem that can be can be solved with  a a little bit of work i would think  okay uh craig says can you talk about  the marketing dispute system that you  use in conjunction with the results of  the exam system  students disputing results  uh can you can you repeat that one more  time can you talk about the marketing  dispute system that you use in  conjunction with the results of the  exam system students disputing results  um we haven't done anything  sophisticated yet for the most part the  students  send the instructor an email and the  instructor  looks at the issue originally  when we wrote the questions yeah there  were a few questions  where the answer key was incorrect um  and we've we've corrected those  yeah for them for the most part here i  think this is part of the philosophy  behind  it that things that require  certain interpretation we expect the  students to  show their work in a certain way that's  not really what this  system is designed to do  anything that that has some subjective  evaluation to that we leave that in the  instructor's hands  this really is can you correctly  calculate an answer to this and  for the most part um there really isn't  a whole lot to dispute  if the answer to this question is the  square root of three over two then  that's what the answer is  so at this point yeah we're not trying  to use mathematica  as a discussion conversation  dispute resolution tool we rely on our  lms and our email system to  to handle those sorts of questions  okay matt says  you said you use the manipulates are  those flash based and if so  how do you deal with compatibility  issues  now that you mention it yeah we have not  used the manipulate recently  and now that i hear that question i  believe that that is exactly the issue  that i think we started running into the  problems because yes i believe that they  were  flash based and then that was the  you know that was the drawback was that  we were  we were having trouble with the  manipulates working on on  various platforms so um yes  good question and now that i think about  it more deeply  i think i think that is an issue that  that we never did resolve that that i  believe that they were flash based  and i think we've abandoned them for the  time being because of the  compatibility issues  craig says are students allowed to use  alternative technologies during exam  taking  such as calculators we are  moving toward um  making as much use of technology in the  classroom  as possible so um  we are basing more of our grade  on things that they do  in discussions in class and a lesser  percentage of the grade on  high stakes pencil and paper exams  um we do use mastery based grading and  this  this i think has been one of the best  features of  an automated system is that rule is very  simple in my class  your homework score needs to be one  hundred percent if your homework score  is not a hundred percent by the end of  the semester  your grade is f you must eventually get  every homework question correct  they can try them as many times as they  want they can come to office hours they  get help  students actually get done with this  stuff but now they  they know what's at stake and so they  spend time making sure that they know  how to do the  um how to do the computational stuff  um no one else just posted anything so  okay and my 30 minutes is uh  just about up here so once again i would  like to  thank everybody for for attending here  and i'm looking forward to continuing  this discussion  you
 

